    "narrative": "The engine for Ghost Ride the WHIP today is **Corbin Carroll**, who leads our starting unit with a robust 77.03 efficiency score against Jeffrey Springs. Despite the natural penalty of a same-handed matchup, **Corbin Carroll** gains significant momentum from a favorable park factor and a premium spot in the order. He is joined at the top of the hierarchy by **James Wood**, who commands a 74.75 score; **James Wood** benefits from an elite 0.430 xwOBA profile and a massive +14% park boost, making him our primary candidate for offensive production. **Tyler Soderstrom** also surges into the top tier (70.82) due to a plum matchup against Mitch Bratt, whose 7.04 xERA provides the softest landing spot of the day.\n\nWe are keeping a close eye on the base paths, particularly with **Brice Turang** (66.10). His value is buoyed not just by his position in the heart of the order, but by a 92% SB-friendly delivery rate from Freddy Peralta, which makes him essential for our scarcity-driven strategy. Meanwhile, the most difficult decision on the bench involved **Josh Naylor** (62.78). He narrowly missed the cut behind **Austin Riley** (63.87); while **Josh Naylor** offers power, the optimizer opted for **Austin Riley**\u2019s superior SP-skill matchup (+14%) to maximize our run-scoring potential. \n\nManagers should note that every player in today's lineup remains \"Lineup Pending,\" introducing a degree of volatility if managers shuffle these hitters down the order. We are comfortable with the floor of **Zach McKinstry** (53.21) because of the wind-aided boost and the relative weakness of Jameson Taillon, but he remains our most volatile start. As we balance the daily game cap, we have strictly adhered to the 40.0 floor, leaving **Travis Bazzana** (39.94) on the pine as the math confirms his matchup against Joe Ryan\u2014who possesses a significantly sharper profile than our other targets\u2014is simply too risky to warrant an active spot today.",
